On Sat, 17 Oct 2026 00:10:55 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> Okay I think this is ready to implement.

Thanks. The implementation is posted as "[PATCH 0/2]
jails/instances/lab-mail: Build the list's mail hub" [1], on b/dev.
It replaces smtprelay and the fetch job with OpenSMTPD, Dovecot over
LMTP, and a two-way mbsync sync.

Building it turned up two places where the spec was wrong, so patch 1
revises the spec before patch 2 implements it:
- LMTP alone files a post only into INBOX. A Sieve script from
  Pigeonhole (mail/dovecot-pigeonhole) puts it into lists, and keeps
  Delivered-To as lab-devel@p7x.dev.
- Dovecot answers OK to a reader's STORE and EXPUNGE and changes
  nothing. It does not refuse them. Acceptance step 7 now expects
  that. MOVE and APPEND still answer NO.

I tested Dovecot, the ACL, LMTP delivery, the sync, and the message
check in a rootless sandbox built from the same packages. smtpd needs
root, so smtpd.conf is checked only with smtpd -n. The cover letter
lists what still needs the acceptance test on beastie.

To build it, you need the three secrets and the empty data
directories that the setup section of README.org lists. If the relay
jail already fetched mail into /jails/data/lab-mail/mail, empty that
first. Otherwise the first sync, with no state, copies every message
to both sides again.

The clients on beastie and lab-claude are left out on purpose. Moving
them retires the jj maildir that lab-process-mail runs on, and the
spec gives its new loop a spec of its own. I suggest building the hub
and running the acceptance steps that need no client change first.
Then I can do the lab-process-mail spec and the clients together.

